#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/id #uuid "d833825d-4ac4-556d-95a1-bb328d5b5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4149" :question/problem "If $\\log_2(x^2-6)=\\log_2(10),$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡(10) \\\\ {x}^{2}-6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡({4}^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡((-4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, we conclude that the solutions are $x = \\pm 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "088cff27-4c06-542c-876b-d3d2ed7b0504" :question/math-academy-id "e-4150" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\ln (2x^2)=\\ln (3x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x =\\dfrac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115043 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "d164cff6-3d50-57c8-a118-8681aaf0c1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4151" :question/problem "Solve $\\log (x-1)=\\log (2x+1)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2x+1 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-2-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-3) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\log (-3)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e2764-cae5-5097-b0e0-4013ce39bd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538277} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc865a8-a99f-55ed-b80f-d011253dcedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538298} {:answer/id #uuid "3b88005d-c042-5ac4-a802-6c3fbb160726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538354} {:answer/id #uuid "71fef0a9-d275-50cc-a7a9-2d08bb857707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538486} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c6624-a345-5138-8ba0-fd272edd299d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc865a8-a99f-55ed-b80f-d011253dcedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538298} :answer-field/id #uuid "f312c34a-9866-5b7f-a9d8-bf68bba44d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58031be4-0ee8-501f-8ac5-6ad86baa2337" :question/math-academy-id "q-100706" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158e7b7b-60f5-5990-bc18-427a015d564c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538279} {:answer/id #uuid "1833719e-2072-5e7a-9cb5-e37a2b744edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538286} {:answer/id #uuid "1e99bf88-1c60-5d1b-909b-f801215f85e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538299} {:answer/id #uuid "3eed8eca-f634-5b53-8c80-0a12204b646e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538361} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6b80b7-ffa9-575d-84f9-149ac8826313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d6b80b7-ffa9-575d-84f9-149ac8826313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538393} :answer-field/id #uuid "d66b6ac8-cb80-5f1e-bd76-69c52b0bc30b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5004d37a-89b2-53f7-9b0b-a8e11a406464" :question/math-academy-id "q-100710" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12),$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12) \\\\ {x}^{2}-52 & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =64 \\\\ x & =\\pm 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2}-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=8$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-8)^{2}-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-8$ is a valid solution.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"$x=\\pm 8.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0eb3cf-c6e5-5bf9-ac8b-d3dd54d57971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538352} {:answer/id #uuid "4063b761-cd24-5cca-b104-08fd4033a09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538364} {:answer/id #uuid "40857b1b-1572-5b34-92b8-97d1eb5df1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538365} {:answer/id #uuid "49eabf9e-e837-5074-8886-edeb3c8178db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538386} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecfd73e-6943-5d3b-8d3c-59b28a93825d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4063b761-cd24-5cca-b104-08fd4033a09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538364} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2dd1e83-f10a-5da1-a48f-8f170a51b0ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6d686ab-e99a-50e7-9f05-fa963ea40c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100713" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65),$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65) \\\\ {x}^{2}-56 & =65 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =121 \\\\ x & =\\pm 11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=11$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({11}^{2}-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(121-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=11$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-11$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-11)^{2}-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(121-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-11$ is a valid solution.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"$x=\\pm 11.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79de5745-5bf0-555e-a601-3b494c6bd938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538509} {:answer/id #uuid "8be64e16-04ad-51d8-827e-a16e9b00296b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538560} {:answer/id #uuid "a1508db0-4863-5e06-8a60-94207ae22916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} {:answer/id #uuid "add9a8e9-c820-5de7-a37b-45ae258e7857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538642} {:answer/id #uuid "b7237201-cb21-5414-84de-7f2cfcb1a828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1508db0-4863-5e06-8a60-94207ae22916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a61e66-3aca-536b-aa8a-9b7a2020142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecdce78b-6ad1-5fcc-9999-f701ae74931a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100773" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7)={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+7 & =2{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}=-1$ has no real solutions. Therefore, our original logarithmic equation has no solutions either."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a641869-5b2a-5c93-8fec-70c216419ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538258} {:answer/id #uuid "1007ddbe-c62c-5841-8280-8935b703fe12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538271} {:answer/id #uuid "27ac4eb7-866d-591b-99fd-4e95d4aa9bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538314} {:answer/id #uuid "6f849d03-4388-5222-bb73-a0d236018103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538482} {:answer/id #uuid "abc230ef-4240-534b-bd00-d63ca4cdb3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ac4eb7-866d-591b-99fd-4e95d4aa9bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538314} :answer-field/id #uuid "b87ce471-30f2-56c2-afaf-a050300cb9c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98df2a56-4669-5921-84a6-4e54451b6791" :question/math-academy-id "q-1131" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35)={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & ={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {v}^{2}+35 & =-12v-1 \\\\ {v}^{2}+12v+36 & =0 \\\\ (v+6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡((-6)^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12(-6)-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡(71) & ={log}_{12}⁡(71).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-6$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09287577-5ed3-56fe-b855-6d26e3c207e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538257} {:answer/id #uuid "31c812be-9c24-5035-bd82-28fd6df10b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538334} {:answer/id #uuid "4a53b6e2-d85d-503b-9005-7384f32b967b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538387} {:answer/id #uuid "6611c66a-d2cb-5b3f-b7f9-124be1499657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538459} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ab6c9e-dc89-5fdb-b731-b962cf7d6239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31c812be-9c24-5035-bd82-28fd6df10b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538334} :answer-field/id #uuid "de40916c-8324-553d-9096-bab0579bf8a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97031323-cb39-5f57-8186-f731d54414db" :question/math-academy-id "q-26532" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{2}⁡(3x+1)={log}_{2}⁡(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}⁡(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace4c8e3-fb3b-5341-aa6e-2abec66da4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112807} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f33d1e-7bd9-53fe-a25c-210684a86ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112808} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2db6ea-6620-5cbb-a2d3-0f5a6ffe63fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112809} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1bf617-7333-54ea-9b16-627401a99811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112810} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9d835b-b38e-5aee-97b2-4478bdfc12c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1f33d1e-7bd9-53fe-a25c-210684a86ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112808} :answer-field/id #uuid "731593c9-3830-55ca-8d4d-43433e4c3f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb4b4f34-a075-5d22-9a44-1bdf761fc15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-27598" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-2)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(8-2x),$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8-2x) \\\\ 3x-2 & =8-2x \\\\ 5x & =10 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(2)-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8-2(2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d6457e-e4a0-59c7-be29-25d96d323f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112886} {:answer/id #uuid "62fb77db-1716-5e1c-adbf-0e1e8e5f53cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112888} {:answer/id #uuid "63a717c9-2d7b-5aa3-8ec6-46509fb70493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112889} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3d225d-49b0-5ea5-9d45-ebcfdc91eb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538296} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd21e92-2701-513e-b783-bd67ac5e1d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3d225d-49b0-5ea5-9d45-ebcfdc91eb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538296} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c92eaa-ba6d-57da-8071-755590fc98d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86cf2399-57e1-58b5-a6e2-83995f27f6e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-27719" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7),$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ {x}^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-4)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"$x=\\pm 4.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371b1475-55c5-532f-b34a-de18250ba6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538346} {:answer/id #uuid "63b5b7ee-a0bf-5cce-8cd4-ef0e8b697d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538454} {:answer/id #uuid "792df15e-6b0d-5e4d-9e48-244de04a293b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538507} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4beebd-bb27-5b86-b030-66ca40a0d99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538707} {:answer/id #uuid "dd22f80c-0bb5-52dd-af6a-2e0500564142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "371b1475-55c5-532f-b34a-de18250ba6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538346} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9bc71c7-9b59-5c65-af5a-72086d309520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41f4ba90-bf6f-5f13-ae98-77405268cba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-27725" :question/problem "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9) \\\\ 2x-1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 4x & =-10 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-\\frac{5}{2})-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6(-\\frac{5}{2})+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-6) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c346939-819e-56f9-9e9d-bad5be5003b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538262} {:answer/id #uuid "52306a77-dde4-5acb-b5ef-6d1b99a085ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538409} {:answer/id #uuid "5a947b84-8ada-55d3-ad03-49d9532b991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538429} {:answer/id #uuid "91b13347-8026-55f4-a9cb-9cb2fff7d699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538574} {:answer/id #uuid "c1987a3b-528f-539a-be0c-b67b5536db72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a947b84-8ada-55d3-ad03-49d9532b991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538429} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ab10ac-4174-5934-8818-7632587d49cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b409a03d-9253-56f1-9515-c6248febada5" :question/math-academy-id "q-27729" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x)={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & ={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+19x & =10x-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+8) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-1)^{2}+19(-1)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-1)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-18) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-18)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-18)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-8)^{2}+19(-8)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-8)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-88) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-88)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-88)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-8$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"No solutions.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "214f33be-dd77-5c99-978a-69ed8f3e00a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112893} {:answer/id #uuid "5fab78db-230a-5484-967e-c80656989093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538442} {:answer/id #uuid "72363bff-e917-5f5a-8dae-8c8430b26d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538488} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5402e8-2482-5ba6-ac79-a9aec1cbc11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538703} {:answer/id #uuid "e972a2a6-8440-546c-a253-3f8622a430a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fab78db-230a-5484-967e-c80656989093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538442} :answer-field/id #uuid "a012673f-43e4-57ba-8170-5e5ff3cd8c03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbd430f4-5fdc-53a4-ae9b-7eaab93363e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-27742" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2 & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2 & =0 \\\\ (2x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{3}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{3}{2})$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {(2)}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot (2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=2$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5462a3aa-9f86-54d4-a352-bd6bd6a53f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538416} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca94c7e-8c18-523a-a529-a59111071887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538475} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dd94fc-f52b-5a46-8d85-41edcdfcc5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538650} {:answer/id #uuid "e47810c3-4772-5842-a46b-a852fe7cbcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538754} {:answer/id #uuid "f62fef4f-0550-5c7e-a11f-4215b6ba7721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e47810c3-4772-5842-a46b-a852fe7cbcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538754} :answer-field/id #uuid "757499ed-7641-5b16-b0bb-95a0bd9e7b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e549fe4-3be0-5aa2-9b34-f060b6793b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-27743" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x-4)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+2),$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x-4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+2) \\\\ 7x-4 & =5x+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7(3)-4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5(3)+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(17) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5925167-6cda-59d6-ab2b-6194304ed60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114584} {:answer/id #uuid "e5cfba55-e9b6-5c5f-b0bf-587a4009dc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114585} {:answer/id #uuid "10f17394-2c6b-5733-9ef4-49a36503fc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114586} {:answer/id #uuid "24ba4a43-0446-5df8-b2fa-656d0181511c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114587} {:answer/id #uuid "2c168775-d75a-54c9-8cb6-f5004cefdf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c168775-d75a-54c9-8cb6-f5004cefdf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114588} :answer-field/id #uuid "b13a9ecb-549f-52e6-b4d3-af0b2d6d8c09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c08455a9-1079-5f41-8296-5e8110d91a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66761" :question/problem "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2) \\\\ 2x & =3x+2 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(-2)+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-4)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fe331b-623c-552e-9c20-0255b34584e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538414} {:answer/id #uuid "75580bdf-e42f-542a-b4aa-5db9e8a21d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538494} {:answer/id #uuid "85689f35-93ad-5798-b798-e9f1e7536146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538539} {:answer/id #uuid "910dd9c5-7c4a-5d17-a062-a66f81d657dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538571} {:answer/id #uuid "d303cba2-df7f-5aef-a154-da6afc090924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75580bdf-e42f-542a-b4aa-5db9e8a21d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538494} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1b6915c-b642-5726-bac9-d65678766025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b0b21ed-9399-541e-870b-dc048c3a5954" :question/math-academy-id "q-79867" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{3}⁡(x+4)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡({25}^{1/2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x+4 & =5 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(1+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00761d95-a4d4-5ce4-8a7e-5d35175bb892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538236} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc5ead2-d9c3-5f69-9e58-d7c2e6b55c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538355} {:answer/id #uuid "6c88bdaf-a69f-5acf-8c06-2c2f997d3c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538474} {:answer/id #uuid "8610c77a-9a30-5662-bc9a-eb87a713ad40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538541} {:answer/id #uuid "8e785bed-bb8c-53b7-b495-b0a5fd57b676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c88bdaf-a69f-5acf-8c06-2c2f997d3c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538474} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc51f3bc-520a-5e97-9ed8-5e68d0470efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "325f155d-1090-569d-9381-79bbdc699aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79872" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-3)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(-3)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((2)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3,2.$"}]}
